  "query_preview": "..(This Evening Through Saturday)\nIssued at 158 PM MDT Sat Oct 3 2026\n\n\nCurrent water vapor imagery shows an upper-level trough moving east \nacross South Dakota pulling this morning's showers eastward as they \ncontinue to fizzle out. 16Z surface analysis depicts a cold front \npushing south through the SD plains into northern parts of Nebraska. \nWind gusts up to 40mph are expected across the SD plains through the \nafternoon. Temperatures will stay in the mid/upper 70s through the \nweek\n\n\nAs the aforementioned trough moves out, zonal to northwest flow will \npersist for the next several days. Temperatures through the week \nwill remain seasonably in the 70s, aside for Monday and Tuesday \nwhich will jump to widespread mid 80s as minor ridging aloft moves \nin temporarily. Temps will then return back to normal for the \nremainder of next week. Tuesday looks slightly breezy with 25mph \ngusts over most of the area. Small shortwaves may crest the ridge \nthrough the week, but the large-scale pattern will break Sun-Mon \nwhen a large trough is forecasted to bring more active weather. \nCurrent guidance has it reaching the west coast Sat and the better\nforcing reaching SD later Monday morning.",
